#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "e31ad4b2-40e2-5e36-95c0-7a961c53f302" :question/math-academy-id "e-21110" :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = 6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = -3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = (1+t^2)\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + e^{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants $c_1$ and $c_2,$ the linear combination \n\n$$\nc_{1}\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)+c_{2}\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + (-1) \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=6$ and $c_2=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=-3$ and $c_2=4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "f74662d2-1414-5716-9eee-2aba2ab7b0fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21111" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n\n- $4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n\n- $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t),$ then $\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t),$ $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t),\\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\nc_1 \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k\\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Matrix Given Solutions to a Matrix ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "ceb2d89c-fd0b-599f-a5ef-91e4be0d487a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21112" :question/problem "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/32e9cba3d7ee75019489dcc8a9c82f1b854bfa77af49e22fa9f51d4412cba95d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/dc64b0c557c838cf31e652ebdb8eee91d1fee8c947b343b16fceaf765c1f8517.png) are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/64b157c381c05102b51320592e61ffff5c4d611af4bd15679f1f2b38f3d01b10.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\nc\\mathbf{u}(t) + d\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w}(t)=4\\mathbf{u}(t)+6\\mathbf{v}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t).$ So, in order to find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ we just need to find $\\mathbf{w}'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4{e}^{4t})^{′} \\\\ (6{e}^{5t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16{e}^{4t} \\\\ 30{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Matrix Given Solutions to a Matrix ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e08d4574-31e2-5629-97bd-f0aa571dc07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353244} {:answer/id #uuid "f55a169a-a77a-50f0-9971-6911e60949bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353245} {:answer/id #uuid "9b83db0f-c714-5ce3-8aa5-e7f2468d9420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353246} {:answer/id #uuid "34230321-2e6c-5d64-b8a5-e5fd90724e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353247} {:answer/id #uuid "cff8eef0-5791-5a12-97a6-d7830fe6b12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b83db0f-c714-5ce3-8aa5-e7f2468d9420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353246} :answer-field/id #uuid "962da458-6f6b-5aba-89de-48364475a6f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ff5758f-22cd-54b1-923c-320e4412a990" :question/math-academy-id "q-168100" :question/problem "If\n$u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nare solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nw(t)=u(t)-v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a83b23-35d4-5f08-ae81-58326f12cd9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353251} {:answer/id #uuid "685a0f61-5846-5fdc-a9f1-2b2551abb6bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353252} {:answer/id #uuid "da1cbd6a-d100-525d-8c47-d5e0a04f9664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353253} {:answer/id #uuid "6259ed36-0c07-5329-a1bc-bc38bd7017da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353254} {:answer/id #uuid "afbcbe14-05f3-5ce2-b005-307783d3d854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "685a0f61-5846-5fdc-a9f1-2b2551abb6bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353252} :answer-field/id #uuid "211127a3-7555-5b9b-9d49-60482c6b0cda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fe51e55-362f-5884-8ef8-5903c807d0bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321646" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-t\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88940947-6990-58cc-8351-aa7b410504a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164065} {:answer/id #uuid "b8631756-4d60-54ed-8af3-c245da4b179e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164066} {:answer/id #uuid "d06558f1-91de-5b73-b834-9ce24df1aa8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164067} {:answer/id #uuid "2609ca6e-ca57-5060-9b3a-686c8a2b572f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164068} {:answer/id #uuid "673eb9c9-74e2-5a4a-b756-d656f4ed00e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2609ca6e-ca57-5060-9b3a-686c8a2b572f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164068} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1d9fe0-6d54-5dc4-ae06-3959252eb8af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2754510-2110-5629-8492-795ca0ada54d" :question/math-academy-id "q-330290" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0cc2535-a6ef-52a0-8674-85514eef50df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353258} {:answer/id #uuid "88df457a-dd56-52e2-8b21-2679acf71996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353259} {:answer/id #uuid "96c61a3d-66f4-5706-957b-f4bf49bff2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353260} {:answer/id #uuid "4048de4a-7d9f-56c6-be50-cfcd28776e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353261} {:answer/id #uuid "1121b58e-ffe1-5b21-94fc-04cf3b8000ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96c61a3d-66f4-5706-957b-f4bf49bff2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353260} :answer-field/id #uuid "7185c857-c246-515f-8e24-0fe682f37939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9feea13f-3341-5519-8dd7-7a36f01f2708" :question/math-academy-id "q-330298" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $4$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61a6fc3e-485f-563c-9520-eb58ca4d5fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353265} {:answer/id #uuid "9b42ab26-b79b-5948-bb7e-989212da2aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353266} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4b7e97-c045-5308-a98b-4a71d9de71cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353267} {:answer/id #uuid "b63ca0a0-28e5-5d31-99c7-ca82e0c6b244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353268} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2c33-e714-53b2-8815-42d514133561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b63ca0a0-28e5-5d31-99c7-ca82e0c6b244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353268} :answer-field/id #uuid "215cbd70-8106-508f-9f8c-1f924c910939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96b2276b-2ede-5f9f-8421-253f775eebdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-330300" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $5$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a9ef31-d81a-5d29-94f1-639717ba5bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353272} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef3ab3a-cf20-5864-b36a-8c436bd77919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353273} {:answer/id #uuid "63444c23-0b8c-5966-96b0-7a07a2f10e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353274} {:answer/id #uuid "db9f390c-5a1a-5689-ac63-39a4546e4408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353275} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c0785d-d0c0-5c02-a7a0-67a0755b0cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63444c23-0b8c-5966-96b0-7a07a2f10e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353274} :answer-field/id #uuid "33834c01-1af2-5a8b-9328-7e6557e6a592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "612c7011-a3d0-59fc-9cb6-1ca04190ddc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-330305" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225e6222-df8e-5510-965c-d962ace35920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353279} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2f5609-2ad7-5e08-a249-f1063d66ff02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353280} {:answer/id #uuid "a1012c61-9f43-5b54-aab5-cc3dd7ac1d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353281} {:answer/id #uuid "d6988c80-4e7c-5bfe-af41-a040089d83fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353282} {:answer/id #uuid "db3c7141-ac60-51bf-bc2e-c3833e8549fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea2f5609-2ad7-5e08-a249-f1063d66ff02"