t be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1956de25-ad59-568f-84ee-1149b7cf8ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353286} {:answer/id #uuid "da8f500b-8344-5abd-a8c7-9caebaad1d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353287} {:answer/id #uuid "cee8f082-27bb-5ed2-916e-5b923b17797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353288} {:answer/id #uuid "eb167ca0-48c3-5cc2-b372-807306c4e31e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353289} {:answer/id #uuid "74ead065-2ec9-5c75-96e2-e6e0485fb2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cee8f082-27bb-5ed2-916e-5b923b17797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353288} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5eaa75-672a-532b-b418-ef1e6247ed08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0814f18e-835c-59a0-8b26-0c49d19ccb68" :question/math-academy-id "q-330312" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={t}^{2}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=5,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdb2952c-5f17-5b2c-8756-8d503b1f5314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353293} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ec60d4-a94b-59fd-8b34-cdd2421ffbf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353294} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d6cb1-856b-5991-a3a0-aac00b64dcb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353295} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e3e51c-f781-5b4e-b3d2-4601ceb584f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353296} {:answer/id #uuid "c498096a-03d7-5d24-a1b0-6ec951313cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c498096a-03d7-5d24-a1b0-6ec951313cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353297} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e673c40-a490-5768-88c5-b7abb009570e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86bfb809-cae6-520a-a84b-72d5d682cb28" :question/math-academy-id "q-330332" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={e}^{2t}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9e13cd5-6900-5d2e-855e-829ce82e6c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164077} {:answer/id #uuid "b05be18f-87d9-577f-9eb4-3c00a290303e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164078} {:answer/id #uuid "c35789a5-de0c-5348-babd-59e45834d9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164079} {:answer/id #uuid "08ddf56e-46eb-5f62-b85b-0d7f5802be9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164080} {:answer/id #uuid "21bc7045-99fc-5e7f-8081-fbf9814cf8b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35789a5-de0c-5348-babd-59e45834d9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164079} :answer-field/id #uuid "9922e169-0eca-553a-8dca-310a49ced5cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e8067e3-26d8-5336-9a9a-e66c696fa115" :question/math-academy-id "q-330348" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={e}^{t}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\\times {x}_{2}(t)$ (cross-product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the first term is not a constant.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c12237a-0c79-5810-a2b1-140e5cbb712f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353300} {:answer/id #uuid "de49b246-fb4c-5045-b331-530e89a82d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353301} {:answer/id #uuid "652be7cd-4bec-54d4-bd02-b2e57ce97148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353302} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e9bd83-1db9-5dae-b35b-35e148e9e343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353303} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0308f-7f2b-5da3-a9b6-f0de0e676b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de49b246-fb4c-5045-b331-530e89a82d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353301} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1fbd11e-1a82-52c0-8f3c-b20bd4913f95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca8f9356-4beb-53da-a0cd-65d91466d466" :question/math-academy-id "q-330353" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Matrix Given Solutions to a Matrix ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2484cd86-5eea-5ae3-92b1-ce843e049eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353307} {:answer/id #uuid "6db395aa-a393-5a34-a962-b945c05d6d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353308} {:answer/id #uuid "eae769c4-b816-5ce6-aa39-5642f6eb2c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353309} {:answer/id #uuid "597dc9f1-d269-5e9c-8825-1be9e1dc610d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353310} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee3f2c3-efbe-5f8e-a1f9-20441e148f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db395aa-a393-5a34-a962-b945c05d6d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353308} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a5d6ee4-0bb6-503c-b92d-610e4e446b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c904dbc3-34a9-5c4f-87ab-adf9b54f3ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-39726" :question/problem "If $\\,u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=-2u(t)-v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-2{e}^{-t})^{′} \\\\ (-{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Matrix Given Solutions to a Matrix ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70f3b7b9-9d5d-57de-ae3d-70084ee0e08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} {:answer/id #uuid "825fc261-844d-5